# Token budgeting

@lede
Measure once, optimize where it matters. The compiler ships a
character-and-token estimator; the rest is pipeline plumbing.
@end

## The estimator

Add `--report-tokens` to any LLM-mode compile. The compiler prints a
stderr line:

```text
brief: doc.brf -> 1,247 chars, ~340 tokens (cl100k estimate)
```

The estimate uses cl100k_base — OpenAI's tokenizer for GPT-3.5 / GPT-4.
It is best-effort and *not* normative. Other tokenizers will produce
different counts; treat the number as a relative gauge.

```bash
brief compile prompt.brf --target=llm --report-tokens > /dev/null
```

## A measurement loop

Compare two phrasings with one command apiece:

```bash
brief compile original.brf --target=llm --report-tokens > /dev/null
brief compile rewritten.brf --target=llm --report-tokens > /dev/null
```

The deltas tell you whether a rewrite saved tokens, where, and how much.
If the delta is small (< 5 percent), it's noise — the rewrite was probably
neutral. If it's large, you found a real win.

## What costs the most tokens

In rough order, on real-world documents:

@dl
Verbatim JSON code blocks
: A 30-line pretty-printed config can be 300+ tokens. Single-line
  minified is the same data in 60 tokens. JSON minification is on by
  default and is the single largest LLM-mode win.
Tables with separator rows
: Markdown's `| --- |` separator costs roughly 5 tokens per column.
  Brief drops it by default in LLM mode.
Decorative formatting
: Extra blank lines, hard-break backslashes, indentation beyond what the
  parser requires. Compile to LLM and re-read what your prompt actually
  contains.
Long-form prose
: The bulk of most prompts is the prose. There is no compiler trick for
  this — write less.
@end

## What costs little

Headings. Emphasis markers. Inline code spans. Lists. The structural
cues these provide help the LLM parse the document, and the per-token
cost is negligible compared to the comprehension benefit.

You can drop emphasis with `--strip-emphasis` if you measure a specific
prompt and it makes no difference, but the default is to keep them.

## A CI gate

Set a budget. Fail the build if the rendered prompt exceeds it.

```bash
brief compile prompt.brf --target=llm --report-tokens 2>&1 1>/dev/null \
  | awk -F'~' '/tokens/ { gsub(/[^0-9].*/, "", $2); if ($2+0 > 2000) exit 1 }'
```

The same idea works in any CI runner. The estimator output is stable
enough across runs that a simple threshold catches regressions.

## Why a CI gate matters

Prompt sprawl is the LLM equivalent of "we'll fix the dependency tree
later." Token budgets are easy to bust by accident — a single example
table, a few extra paragraphs, an inline JSON config — and the cost
shows up only on the next billing cycle. A CI gate makes the cost visible
at PR time.

## Don't over-optimize

The `--strip-emphasis` flag, dropping `preserve_code_fences`, and dropping
`--keep-table-rule` are all cost-saving levers. None of them are
free — each one removes a structural cue the model uses to parse.

Measure the *quality* of the LLM's output, not just the token count. A
50-token saving that drops accuracy by 10 percent is a bad trade. The
estimator can't tell you that; only your eval can.

## Where the cl100k assumption breaks

Different downstream tokenizers diverge from cl100k for specific token
classes:

- Anthropic's tokenizer for Claude treats whitespace and punctuation
  differently in places.
- Open-weight models (Llama, Mistral) use SentencePiece variants with
  their own quirks.
- Multilingual content costs more in cl100k than in BPE-trained
  tokenizers tuned for the target language.

The compiler's number is still useful for *relative* measurement on the
same source — but if you depend on exact tokenization for a specific
provider, run the provider's tokenizer over the rendered output. The
compiler doesn't pretend to.
